An abstract class in Java can have a constructor, even though you cannot instantiate an abstract class directly.
 This might seem counterintuitive at first, but there’s a solid reason. Let’s break it down clearly.

1. Purpose of a constructor in an abstract class

The constructor in an abstract class is called when a subclass is instantiated.
Think of it as a way to initialize fields or perform setup that is common to all subclasses.

Even though you can’t do:

AbstractClass obj = new AbstractClass(); // ❌ Not allowed

You can do:

class ConcreteClass extends AbstractClass {
    ConcreteClass() {
        super(); // Calls abstract class constructor
    }
}

ConcreteClass obj = new ConcreteClass(); // ✅ Allowed

Here, super() calls the constructor of the abstract class.

2. Why it is useful

Initialize common state: If the abstract class has fields that all subclasses need, you can initialize them in the abstract constructor.

Run common logic: You can execute code that should run whenever any subclass is created.

Support inheritance chain: Java’s constructor chaining still applies. Abstract classes can participate in the chain just like normal classes.

Example:

abstract class Vehicle {
    String brand;

    Vehicle(String brand) {
        this.brand = brand;
        System.out.println("Vehicle constructor called");
    }

    abstract void start();
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    Car(String brand) {
        super(brand);
        System.out.println("Car constructor called");
    }

    void start() {
        System.out.println(brand + " car started");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car car = new Car("Toyota");
    }
}

Output:

Vehicle constructor called
Car constructor called
Toyota car started

✅ Notice how the abstract class constructor executes first, initializing the brand field for the subclass.

3. Key points

Abstract classes can’t be instantiated, but their constructors are used by subclasses.

Constructors in abstract classes help initialize common fields and logic.

If an abstract class has no explicit constructor, Java provides a default no-arg constructor.